<Hero />

## What you'll build

What if you could use the API traffic your application already receives to
create a reusable regression test suite?

That's the idea behind **Keploy**.

In this tutorial, we'll use a small **Go + Echo + PostgreSQL URL shortener** to
see the complete workflow:

```text
Run the application
        ↓
Record real API traffic
        ↓
Generate test cases + mocks
        ↓
Replay the same interactions
        ↓
Verify the responses
````

By the end, you'll have seen Keploy turn real application behaviour into
repeatable API tests — without manually writing every test case or creating
database mocks yourself.

<Callout type="info" title="What you'll build">
  A working Go + Echo URL shortener with PostgreSQL, recorded Keploy test cases,
  automatically captured dependency mocks, and a repeatable replay workflow.
</Callout>

---

## Before we start

### Prerequisites

You'll need:

* Go installed
* Docker installed and running
* `curl`
* Keploy CLI
* A Linux/WSL environment for the native setup used in this tutorial

You don't need prior Keploy experience. We'll explain what each command is
doing and, more importantly, **why we're running it**.

<Callout type="tip" title="New to Keploy?">
  Think of Keploy as a traffic recorder during `record` mode and a replay
  engine during `test` mode. It observes your application's network
  interactions and turns them into reusable test data.
</Callout>

---

## 1. Get the Go application

We'll use Keploy's Go samples repository and its Echo + PostgreSQL URL
shortener.

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/keploy/samples-go.git
cd samples-go/echo-sql
```

Download the Go dependencies:

```bash
go mod download
```

### How the sample works

The application exposes a URL-shortening endpoint:

```text
POST /url
    │
    └── creates a short URL

GET /:id
    │
    └── redirects to the original URL
```

Both operations use PostgreSQL to store and retrieve URL mappings.

<ArchitectureDiagram mode="record" />

<Callout type="info" title="One small configuration detail">
  In the native Linux/WSL setup, PostgreSQL runs inside Docker while the Go
  application runs directly on your machine. Make sure the PostgreSQL host in
  the sample application points to `localhost` for this setup.
</Callout>

---

## 2. Build the application

Once the dependencies are available, build the Go binary:

```bash
go build -o echo-psql-url-shortener
```

You should now have an executable named:

```text
echo-psql-url-shortener
```

<Callout type="tip" title="Why build a binary?">
  Keploy needs a command that can start your application. Using the generated
  binary keeps the record and replay commands simple and reproducible.
</Callout>

---

## 3. Start PostgreSQL

The Echo sample uses PostgreSQL as its database dependency.

Start the PostgreSQL service using the sample's Docker Compose configuration:

```bash
docker-compose up postgres
```

Keep this process running.

In another terminal, verify that PostgreSQL is available:

```bash
docker ps
```

You should see the PostgreSQL container running.

<Callout type="warning" title="Running Docker Compose from the wrong directory?">
  The Compose file belongs to the sample application. Make sure you're inside
  `samples-go/echo-sql` before running the command.
</Callout>

---

## 4. Start Keploy in record mode

This is where Keploy enters the workflow.

Instead of starting the Go application directly, let Keploy start it:

```bash
sudo -E PATH=$PATH keploy record \
  -c "./echo-psql-url-shortener"
```

Keploy now sits between your application and its network interactions while
recording.

<ArchitectureDiagram mode="record" />

### What's actually happening?

Without Keploy:

```text
Client
  │
  ▼
Go + Echo
  │
  ▼
PostgreSQL
```

With Keploy recording:

```text
Client
  │
  ▼
Keploy
  │
  ▼
Go + Echo
  │
  ▼
PostgreSQL
```

Keploy observes the API interaction and the application's communication with
its dependency.

The goal isn't to modify your application code.

Instead, Keploy uses the traffic flowing through the application to generate
the information needed for replay.

<Callout type="tip" title="Think of record mode as teaching Keploy">
  Your real API requests become examples of how your application behaves.
  Keploy captures those interactions so they can be reproduced later.
</Callout>

---

## 5. Generate a test case with a real API request

With the application running in record mode, make an actual API request.

Create a shortened URL:

```bash
curl --request POST \
  --url http://localhost:8082/url \
  --header 'content-type: application/json' \
  --data '{
    "url": "https://github.com"
  }'
```

The application returns a shortened URL.

For example:

```json
{
  "ts": 1786354079923880503,
  "url": "http://localhost:8082/4KepjkTT"
}
```

The exact short ID will be different for your run.

<ResultCard
type="success"
title="First interaction"
value="POST /url"
description="A real API request has now been captured while Keploy is recording."
/>

### Why does this become a test?

Because the request is real.

Instead of guessing what a test should look like, we're giving Keploy an
actual interaction between a client and our application.

That interaction can now be replayed later.

---

## 6. Exercise the database-backed endpoint

The POST request created a short URL.

Now use that generated ID to exercise the application's lookup and redirect
flow.

For example:

```bash
curl http://localhost:8082/4KepjkTT
```

You can also open the same URL in a browser.

The application should redirect you to the original URL.

In our run, the response was:

```text
HTTP/1.1 308 Permanent Redirect
Location: https://github.com
```

<Callout type="tip" title="Why record more than one request?">
  The POST request tests URL creation, while the GET request tests URL
  retrieval and redirection. Together they exercise two different paths
  through the application.
</Callout>

---

## 7. Stop recording and inspect the generated tests

Once you've exercised the endpoints, stop the recording process:

```text
Ctrl + C
```

Keploy stores the generated test cases and mocks in the `keploy/` directory.

Inspect the generated files:

```bash
find keploy -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort
```

Depending on your Keploy version and the traffic you recorded, the exact
filenames can vary.

You should find generated test cases and mock data under the `keploy/`
directory.

<Callout type="info" title="Test cases vs mocks">
  A test case describes the API interaction that should be replayed. A mock
  contains recorded dependency behaviour — such as a database response — that
  Keploy can reproduce during the test.
</Callout>

---

## 8. Inspect a generated test case

A generated test case is stored as YAML.

For example:

```bash
cat keploy/tests/test-1.yaml
```

The exact filename depends on the test cases generated during your run.

Conceptually, the test case contains information such as:

```yaml
method: POST
url: http://localhost:8082/url
```

along with the recorded request and response data.

The important part isn't the YAML syntax itself.

It's the workflow:

```text
Real API request
       ↓
Keploy observes it
       ↓
Test case generated
       ↓
Test case can be replayed
```

<ResultCard
type="success"
title="Generated test"
value="HTTP interaction → YAML test case"
description="The API interaction has been converted into reusable test data."
/>

---

## 9. Inspect the PostgreSQL mock

The redirect request also interacts with PostgreSQL.

During recording, Keploy captures the dependency interaction and stores the
result as mock data.

Depending on the generated test structure, you can inspect the mocks under the
`keploy/` directory.

For example:

```bash
find keploy -iname "*mock*"
```

Conceptually, the interaction looks like:

```text
GET /4KepjkTT
      │
      ▼
  Go + Echo
      │
      ▼
 PostgreSQL
      │
      ▼
Recorded database response
```

During replay, Keploy can provide the recorded dependency response instead of
requiring the exact same live database state.

<ArchitectureDiagram mode="test" />

<Callout type="tip" title="This is where Keploy becomes useful">
  You don't have to manually create a fake PostgreSQL response for every
  recorded interaction. Keploy captures dependency behaviour while recording
  and can reproduce it during replay.
</Callout>

---

## 10. Replay the recorded test cases

Now let's replay what we recorded.

Run:

```bash
sudo -E PATH=$PATH keploy test \
  -c "./echo-psql-url-shortener" \
  --delay 10
```

Keploy starts the application, replays the recorded API interactions, provides
the recorded dependency responses where required, and compares the resulting
responses with the recorded expectations.

The flow now looks like:

<ArchitectureDiagram mode="test" />

```text
Recorded test
      │
      ▼
   Keploy
      │
      ▼
 Go + Echo
      │
      ▼
Recorded dependency behaviour
      │
      ▼
   Response
      │
      ▼
Compare with expected response
```

<ResultCard
type="success"
title="Replay"
value="Record → Replay → Verify"
description="The same application behaviour can now be checked without manually recreating the original interaction."
/>

---

## 11. What just happened?

Let's step back from the commands.

We started with:

```text
A running Go application
```

Then generated:

```text
Real API traffic
      ↓
Keploy record
      ↓
Test cases + dependency mocks
```

And finally replayed them:

```text
Test cases
      ↓
Keploy test
      ↓
Application
      ↓
Response comparison
```

So the complete workflow is:

```text
        RECORD
           │
           ▼
   Real API interaction
           │
           ▼
   Test cases + mocks
           │
           ▼
          TEST
           │
           ▼
     Replay requests
           │
           ▼
    Compare responses
```

That's the core idea behind Keploy's record-and-replay workflow.

---

## 12. Why is this useful for a Go developer?

Imagine you change the URL-shortening logic tomorrow.

You could manually test:

* creating a URL
* retrieving a URL
* redirecting to the original URL
* database interactions
* response formats

Or you could replay the interactions you already captured.

That's the value of turning real traffic into reusable tests:

**your application's existing behaviour becomes a starting point for
regression testing.**

<Callout type="info" title="The bigger picture">
  Recording isn't the end goal. The goal is to create a repeatable safety net
  that you can run whenever your application changes.
</Callout>

---

## 13. A simple mental model

If you remember only two commands from this tutorial, remember these:

```bash
keploy record
```

**Capture real application behaviour.**

And:

```bash
keploy test
```

**Replay that behaviour and verify the result.**

```text
record
  ↓
capture
  ↓
generate
  ↓
replay
  ↓
verify
```

---

## 14. From local testing to CI

The workflow we've built locally can become part of a larger development
workflow.

A typical team workflow could look like:

```text
Developer changes code
        ↓
CI pipeline starts
        ↓
Keploy tests run
        ↓
API responses are compared
        ↓
Regression detected?
     ↙       ↘
   Yes        No
   ↓           ↓
Fix code    Ship safely
```

This is where recorded API tests become more valuable than a one-time local
experiment: they can become part of your application's regression-testing
workflow.

---

## Summary

You've now gone from a running Go application to a reusable API test workflow.

You:

* Set up the Echo + PostgreSQL sample
* Built the Go application
* Started Keploy in record mode
* Generated real API traffic
* Captured test cases and dependency behaviour
* Inspected the generated test data
* Replayed the recorded interactions
* Understood how the workflow can support regression testing

The mental model to keep is:

```text
Record → Inspect → Replay → Verify
```

**Real traffic becomes reusable test behaviour.**

And that's the simplest way to understand Keploy.

